  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 1242 AM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nSaturday and Sunday...A cold front pushes south into eastern CO \nSaturday morning, while the southern closed low moves up across AZ \nand reaches the Four Corners by Sat night. This will produce strong \nsouth to southwest flow, this time situated across the eastern half \nof the state. Widespread showers and thunderstorms are expected on \nSaturday, with excessive rainfall amounts possible for the eastern \nplains, and new accumulating snow for the mts as snow levels drop to \naround 9000 ft late that night. SPC currently has just painted much \nof the plains in General Thunder, but I would not be surprised to \nsee a Marginal enter the plains on upcoming updates due to \nimpressive shear. On Sunday the upper low weakens slightly and \ntracks east into CO, continuing to bring rain and storm chances to \nthe plains, and additional light snow to the mts. This will be the \ncoolest days of the forecast period, with highs in the upper 40s to \nmid 50s for the high valleys both days. For the plains, look for 50s \nto around 60F for Saturday, then 50s for Sunday.\n\nMonday through Thursday...Upper low weakens, opens and tracks east \nacross CO as an open wave on Monday, with just some lingering \nisolated to low end scattered showers across the CWA. Temporary \nbroad ridging aloft moves across the region for Tuesday, then brisk \nsouthwest flow aloft from a disturbance passing by to the north for \nWednesday and Thursday. Plan on a gradual warming and drying trend \nthrough the week, with a return to seasonal temperatures. Moore",
